<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    Nop.Web - fifth stage of the migration and the public storefront web application.
    Downstream of Nop.Core, Nop.Data, Nop.Services and Nop.Web.Framework, and a
    parallelizable SIBLING of Nop.Admin (neither references the other - design section 6).
    (Requirements 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 2.4, 4.1)

    SCOPE OF TASK 7.1: this file, the package graph and the project references ONLY.
    No C# file, no .cshtml view, no Global.asax and no Web.config was touched - those are
    tasks 7.2 (hosting model), 7.3 (controllers/views/routing), 7.4 (configuration) and
    7.5 (obsolete-file removal). The project is EXPECTED not to compile at the end of 7.1;
    the surviving errors are the CS*/RZ* inventory those tasks consume.

    ===================================================================================
    WHY Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web
    ===================================================================================
    Unlike Nop.Web.Framework (which has zero .cshtml files and therefore stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk - see the long note in that project), Nop.Web owns 193 .cshtml
    views under Views\ and Themes\DefaultClean\Views\. The Web SDK imports the Razor SDK,
    supplies the ASP.NET Core app host targets, and implicitly adds the
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App FrameworkReference.

    NOTE: there is deliberately NO explicit <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    below. Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web adds it implicitly, and restating it fails the build with
    NETSDK1086. This is the one place the Nop.Web.Framework pattern must NOT be copied.

    The TFM is the OS-agnostic net10.0 and deliberately NOT net10.0-windows: the storefront
    has no System.Drawing / ACL / WindowsIdentity surface of its own (verified - zero hits
    for System.Drawing, ImageResizer and System.Runtime.Caching anywhere under Nop.Web
    excluding Administration\). The Windows-only residue in the solution is confined to
    Nop.Web.Framework's Security\FilePermissionHelper.cs, which Nop.Web merely CALLS - see
    runtime-deferrals.md section 18.4 for the CA1416 warnings that call chain will surface
    at task 7.3.

    ===================================================================================
    THE ONE STRUCTURAL TRAP IN THIS PROJECT: Administration\ IS A NESTED PROJECT
    ===================================================================================
    Nop.Admin does not live beside Nop.Web - it lives INSIDE it, at
    src\Presentation\Nop.Web\Administration\Nop.Admin.csproj. In the classic project this
    was harmless because every compiled file was listed explicitly. Under SDK implicit
    globbing it is not: the default Compile/Content/None globs would swallow Nop.Admin's
    277 .cs files and 325 .cshtml views into Nop.Web, producing a nonsense compilation
    (duplicate types, two DependencyRegistrars, admin views compiled into the storefront)
    and pre-empting task 8. Measured: 508 .cs files under this directory, of which only
    231 belong to Nop.Web; 518 .cshtml, of which only 193 belong to Nop.Web.

    DefaultItemExcludes below is therefore load-bearing, not hygiene. It is used in
    preference to per-glob <Compile Remove=... /> items because it applies uniformly to
    every default glob the SDK and the Razor SDK derive - Compile, Content (which is where
    .cshtml and **\*.config land), EmbeddedResource and None - so no future SDK glob can
    leak the admin tree back in.

    Plugins\** is excluded for the same reason even though the directory does not exist in
    source control: PluginManager shadow-copies discovered plugin assemblies into
    ~\Plugins\bin at startup, and deployed plugin folders carry their own .cshtml and
    .config files that must never be part of Nop.Web's own compilation.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);Administration\**;Plugins\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
  </PropertyGroup>

